  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Late summer heat+humidity to close August and \nopen September\n\nStrong upper ridge expanding east-northeastward from the \nsouth central Plains through the TN Valley into the southern \nMid Atlantic and Southeast states will deliver a stretch of \nabove normal late summer warmth and very humid conditions to \nclose out August and kickoff September. Forecast max/min \ntemperatures are projected to rise several degrees above the \nhistorical average Sunday-Thursday with the largest departure \nfrom normal (+10-20F) found in midweek overnight lows. The late \nsummer heat is forecast to peak Tuesday and Wednesday with max \nheat indices approaching advisory levels in the mid to upper 90s\nacross parts of south central PA. The well above normal \ntemperatures combined with high humidity and very warm overnight\nlows will result in level 2 (moderate) to level 3 (major) \nHeatRisk values Tuesday and Wednesday. Increasing troughing \nacross the Northeast U.S. should drop temperatures back toward \nseasonal climo by Friday/Saturday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Bouts of heavy rain and severe storms possible\nthrough late week \n\nA series of mid level impulses tracking eastward along the \nnorthern periphery of the elongated 500mb ridge will interact \nwith anomalous moisture/instability and strong flow aloft to \nproduce a few episodes of heavy rainfall and possible severe \nweather through late week. There are typical timing and \namplitude differences of the smaller scale disturbances that \nwill need to be resolved, but the overall convective signal from\noperational/ensemble/ML/AI guidance is supportively bullish. \nThere is a growing signal for severe storms and heavy rain on \nTuesday and Wednesday. SPC introduced a level 2 severe t-storm \nrisk for D3/Tuesday and maintained the 15% or level 2 equivalent\nrisk for D4/Wednesday. WPC continues to cover parts of CPA with\nlevel 1 excessive rainfall outlooks which may be refined/upgraded\nas the convective details become more clear and confidence in \ntiming and location increases.",
